{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc | sed -n '1710,1755p'; rg -n 'store-put|active-module|store-of' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | head -25; nl -ba collab/1790974400000-architect-python-c4-design.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '20,140p'\"","aggregated_output":"  1710\t     (py/make-function \"keys\" {:params [\"self\"], :no-kw true} [] []\n  1711\t                       (fn [args] (py/list (get (cell/get (py/arg args 0)) :keys))))]\n  1712\t    [py.b/dict-values\n  1713\t     (py/make-function \"values\" {:params [\"self\"], :no-kw true} [] []\n  1714\t                       (fn [args] (py/list (get (cell/get (py/arg args 0)) :vals))))]\n  1715\t    [py.b/dict-get\n  1716\t     (py/make-function \"get\" {:params [\"self\" \"key\" \"default\"], :no-kw true} [:py/None] []\n  1717\t                       (fn [args]\n  1718\t                         (if (py/dict-has? (py/arg args 0) (py/arg args 1))\n  1719\t                           (py/getitem (py/arg args 0) (py/arg args 1))\n  1720\t                           (py/arg args 2))))]\n  1721\t    [py.b/next\n  1722\t     (py/make-function \"next\" {:params [\"iterator\" \"default\"], :no-kw true} [:py/missing] []\n  1723\t                       (fn [args] (py/next (py/arg args 0) (py/arg args 1))))]\n  1724\t    [py.b/gen-send\n  1725\t     (py/make-function \"send\" {:params [\"self\" \"value\"], :no-kw true} [] []\n  1726\t                       (fn [args] (py/gen-send (py/arg args 0) (py/arg args 1))))]])\n  1727\t\n  1728\t\n  1729\t(def ^:private state-definitions\n  1730\t  \"Definitions that allocate: the two runtime cells, the builtin classes\n  1731\t   and the builtin functions. These are the only prelude forms that run at\n  1732\t   load.\"\n  1733\t  (-> '[[py.rt/handlers (cell/new nil)]\n  1734\t        [py.rt/out (cell/new [])]]\n  1735\t      (into (map (fn [[nm key base]]\n  1736\t                   [key (cond->> (list 'py/make-class nm (or base :py/None))\n  1737\t                          (= \"StopIteration\" nm) (list 'py/stop-iteration-class))]))\n  1738\t            builtin-classes)\n  1739\t      (into builtin-function-definitions)))\n  1740\t\n  1741\t\n  1742\t(def function-definitions\n  1743\t  \"Every prelude function as `[store-key form]`, in definition order.\"\n  1744\t  core-definitions)\n  1745\t\n  1746\t\n  1747\t(defn- definitions->uast\n  1748\t  [defs]\n  1749\t  (u/seq-nodes (map (fn [[k form]] (u/def! k (u/sexp->uast form))) defs)))\n  1750\t\n  1751\t\n  1752\t(def functions-uast\n  1753\t  \"Only the function definitions: defining lambdas runs nothing, so this\n  1754\t   loads on any composition, with or without cells.\"\n  1755\t  (definitions->uast function-definitions))\n151:(defn store-put\n153:   `:vm/store-put` effect, and the four direct store instructions write\n167:;; A linked module's closures carry `:yin.k/store-of m`, the manifest\n169:;; positional kernels hold the running body's `:store-of` as a register,\n176:(def store-of-key\n179:  :yin.k/store-of)\n182:(defn env-store-of\n186:  (get env store-of-key))\n189:(defn without-store-of\n193:  (if (contains? env store-of-key) (dissoc env store-of-key) env))\n198:   positional kernels' `:store-of` register, else the named kernels'\n201:  (or (:store-of state) (env-store-of (:env state))))\n206:   closure runs (`store-of` non-nil), its module store, with no ambient\n209:  [state store-of]\n210:  (if (some? store-of)\n211:    (get (:module-stores state) store-of {})\n217:   `store-of` names, or the task's own store at top level. Both go\n218:   through `store-put`, so a reserved key is refused either way.\"\n219:  [state store-of key val]\n220:  (if (some? store-of)\n221:    (update-in state [:module-stores store-of]\n222:               (fn [module-store] (store-put (or module-store {}) key val)))\n223:    (assoc state :store (store-put (:store state) key val))))\n694:   its body resolves against: its own `:store-of`, or `own` when it has\n720:                      m (or (get marker store-of-key) own)]\n    20\t\n    21\t## 1. Python imports on the landed linker\n    22\t\n    23\t**Module shape.** A Python unit compiled as module `a.b` becomes one linker module:\n    24\t\n    25\t```clojure\n    26\t(do (require 'py)            ; hoisted: code delivery only\n    27\t    (require 'pym.c)         ; one per static import in the unit\n    28\t    (yin/def spec {:name \"a.b\" :package? false})\n    29\t    (yin/def body (fn [%globals %globals-fn] <lowered module body>)))\n    30\t;; :yin.module/exports #{spec body}\n    31\t```\n    32\t\n    33\t- **Requires are hoisted and pinned.** Every static import becomes a module-level `(require 'pym.x)`. Installs are pure, so hoisting is invisible to Python; it puts free names like `pym.c/body` in the shape step 5a already discharges, and `:yin.module/requires` pins each dependency by manifest address. The pins are derivable from the rows, so no new manifest key.\n    34\t- **Execution stays lazy.** `import c` lowers at the statement to `(py/import \"c\" pym.c/spec pym.c/body)` plus the binding. The body runs in the importing task, in statement order, under that task's declared effects.\n    35\t\n    36\t**Resolution order.**\n    37\t\n    38\t1. `sys.modules` (a heap dict in prelude state): a hit returns the module object.\n    39\t2. Runtime-synthesized modules (`sys`, `builtins`), pre-seeded by `py/init!`.\n    40\t3. The linker: task registry hit (the code cache, `module.cljc:530`), else name environment fold → manifest → verify → pure install → receive.\n    41\t4. Refusal: see ImportError below.\n    42\t\n    43\tThere is no `sys.path` and there are no finders. The \"search path\" is the reader's snapshot set and declared principals (`linker.dht.md` 7.1-7.4), consistent with section 9.6: a dependency edge names a content address.\n    44\t\n    45\t**`py/import` semantics (prelude code).** On a miss it creates the module object, sets `__name__` and `__package__`, inserts it into `sys.modules` before running the body, and runs the body. On an exception it removes the entry and re-raises. There is no park inside `py/import`, because code was delivered at link time.\n    46\t\n    47\t**`__name__`, `__main__`, `__package__`.**\n    48\t\n    49\t- The same code image serves both roles. `py/run-main` instantiates it as `\"__main__\"`; `py/import` instantiates it under its dotted name. `if __name__ == '__main__'` needs nothing else, and the code address does not depend on the role.\n    50\t- `__package__` comes from `spec`. Package-ness is source layout, not derivable from rows, so `spec` is legitimate unit metadata.\n    51\t- `__file__` is absent (as for CPython builtin modules); `__spec__` and `__loader__` are `None`.\n    52\t\n    53\t**Relative imports.** Resolved statically at lowering from the unit's declared module name (section 5.1: source units are explicit). A relative import in a unit with no declared package lowers to code that raises `ImportError` at that statement, preserving `try/except` behaviour.\n    54\t\n    55\t**Packages.**\n    56\t\n    57\t- `import a.b.c` requires and imports `a`, `a.b`, `a.b.c` in order and sets each as an attribute on its parent.\n    58\t- `from m import *` is a prelude loop over the ordered key vector into the importer's dict.\n    59\t- Restriction: `from pkg import submodule` does not auto-import the submodule, because lowering cannot know it exists; the support profile records it.\n    60\t\n    61\t**Names.** The registry nests dotted names (`module.cljc:33-74`, `engine.cljc:132-137`), so a module named `a` shadows `a.b` (inferred, untested). Until the linker seat fixes that, no linker module name may be a dotted prefix of another:\n    62\t\n    63\t- base prelude: `py`\n    64\t- hook prelude: `pysp`\n    65\t- Python module `a.b.c`: `pym.a$b$c` (`$` is valid EDN and never in a Python identifier, so the mangling is injective)\n    66\t\n    67\t**Circular imports.** Python-level cycle semantics fall out for free: insert-before-execute, and \"cannot import name\" on a partially initialized module. Code-delivery cycles cannot be pinned by content (a pins b pins a), and the linker refuses `:require-cycle` (`module.cljc:533-537`).\n    68\t\n    69\t- First: refuse at publish with a diagnostic naming the cycle.\n    70\t- Later: the publisher emits one linker module per strongly connected component exporting `body$a`, `body$b`, plus one thin alias module per member that re-exports its body. This uses only landed mechanisms (re-exported closures keep their origin and `:store-of`) and needs no SCC-identity ruling.\n    71\t\n    72\t**ImportError.** A link refusal is raised as the effect's error (`engine.cljc:975-978, 1714`). The VM has no guest-catchable form of it, so `try: import x / except ImportError` cannot work for a module absent at link time. This is the one engine addition C4 needs: a `module/try-require` whose refusal resumes as plain data, the `stream/poll!` precedent (decision 5). Until it lands, an absent dependency refuses the dependent's link before any Python runs, and the support profile says so.\n    73\t\n    74\t**`importlib`.**\n    75\t\n    76\t- Supported: `import_module` with a literal name; `reload`, which re-runs the same image against the same dict (the registry hit answers forever, so new code needs a new name-environment snapshot).\n    77\t- Refused with an explicit unsupported error: finders, loaders, `meta_path`, `path_hooks`, `__import__` override, non-literal names. A non-literal name would be an unpinned dependency and needs a dynamic binding read the engine does not have.\n    78\t\n    79\t## 2. `sys.modules` and the module namespace\n    80\t\n    81\tThe linked module's store does not back the namespace dict.\n    82\t\n    83\t- **Module store:** holds `spec` and `body`, immutable after install.\n    84\t- **Module object:** a heap cell `{:py/type :module :name s :dict d}`, where `d` is the same dict the body receives as `%globals`.\n    85\t- **`setattr`, `del`, `globals()` writes:** dict operations on the heap. The linker's store model (literal keys, no delete, first link wins) is untouched.\n    86\t\n    87\tConsequences:\n    88\t\n    89\t- Two tasks importing one module get independent namespaces, consistent with \"one Python process is one task\".\n    90\t- The open \"cross-task module stores\" decision (`linker.md:2389`) is not needed.\n    91\t- Module state migrates only when heap lift lands, as already true for any Python task.\n    92\t- The link-time `:bindings` snapshot (likely stale after a store write; inferred) never matters, because consumers read only closures and plain data through it.\n    93\t\n    94\t## 3. The linked prelude\n    95\t\n    96\t**Shape.**\n    97\t\n    98\t- **One source, two emitters.** The definition list in `prelude.cljc` stays the single source. The bundled emitter is today's. The module emitter strips the module's own namespace from keys and internal references, because export keys must be bare.\n    99\t- **State behind `py/init!`.** The module-level `state-definitions` (`prelude.cljc:1729-1739`) move into `py/init!`, which writes one state slot in the active module store and is idempotent. Entry wrappers call it; install children never do.\n   100\t- **Builtins become a namespace dict** built by `py/init!`. A global miss falls back to it, which is Python's own model.\n   101\t- **Linked entry wrapper:** `(do (require 'py) (py/init!) (py/run-main (fn [%globals %globals-fn] ...)))`.\n   102\t\n   103\t**What breaks.**\n   104\t\n   105\t- **Addresses change once.** Every golden moves, because the wrapper and the builtin reads change.\n   106\t- **Publish refuses the prelude today.** A free name bound by a host module is `:yin.link.publish/host-module` (`publish.cljc:294-295`), and the prelude calls `cell/*`, `data/*`, later `integer/*`. Linker-seat work is required.\n   107\t- **The AST walker cannot link it.** A module whose exported lambda reads a sibling definition is refused `:undeclared-free` on the tree format (`linker.dht.md:969-975`). Python acceptance is four VMs, so this deferral must lift or the linked profile claims three.\n   108\t- **Safepoint load order inverts.** Today the hook prelude loads first and may only allocate (`safepoint.cljc:18-23`). Linked, `pysp` requires `py`. Its cursor cannot be created at install, and a module closure cannot read the ambient `py.sp/signals` (no ambient fallback, `linker.md:1438-1447`). So the wrapper passes the stream to `(pysp/attach! signals)`.\n   109\t- **Imported modules run uninstrumented.** Site marks live in the frontend-metadata side table, not in rows, so a published tree carries none. A `while True` in imported code is not interruptible until site sets are published.\n   110\t- **Atomicity (8.11) holds, and gets stronger.** The stage never sees prelude rows, and `py/import` adds no park. Decision 6 (\"revisit when linked\") resolves to keep marks: structure still cannot tell a loop from a function.\n   111\t\n   112\t**What gets simpler.**\n   113\t\n   114\t- The lowering drops `builtin-names` and direct `py.b/*` reads; adding a builtin class no longer touches `lower.cljc`.\n   115\t- A user program's address no longer depends on the prelude revision, so the \"prelude grows, every unit's address changes\" notes at `yang.antlr.md:1830` and `:2688` end.\n   116\t- `isinstance` across units in one task works.\n   117\t- The data and integer modules stay host modules (no host function arrives by linking), but the prelude manifest declares them under `:yin.module/primitives` by profile address and step 5b enforces it. That answers the C3-S1 note that `module-version` is inert data.\n   118\t- The hand-kept `host-names` sets become derivable from the tree.\n   119\t\n   120\t**Selectable.** The bundled profile stays selectable until the migration gate: the linked corpus green on four VMs and three hosts. Then delete it; no compat shim.\n   121\t\n   122\t## 4. REPL frontend catalog/SPI\n   123\t\n   124\t**Today.** `yin/repl.cljc` has a closed `case` over `:clojure`, `:python`, `:php` (`:1578-1584`), a `lang-labels` map (`:240`), `:clojure` special cases (`:1587-1596`, `:1693-1697`), and static requires of all three frontends (`:18-20`). Its `:python` is the legacy `yang.python`, not the ANTLR frontend. The ANTLR parser exists only on the JVM (`src/clj/yang/python/antlr/parser.clj`).\n   125\t\n   126\t**Design.**\n   127\t\n   128\t- **`yang.frontend` (new, pure cljc):** manifest validation, `install catalog manifest binding => catalog'`, selection by `[id revision]`. The catalog is a value. A manifest carrying a function or handle is rejected.\n   129\t- **The REPL takes the catalog from its composition** (`create-state` option). Each host's `main` builds it; `yin.repl` requires no frontend. `(lang x)` selects from the session's pinned catalog snapshot. Shell commands remain the shell's own syntax.\n   130\t- **The binding is a pair of stages over supplied streams** (parse, lower) plus an optional completeness probe. The REPL steps them to an outcome. A parser worker or remote service is the same shape, so swapping one in does not change the REPL; a slow service needs a pending-compile state like the existing pending-require.\n   131\t- **No fallback between frontends.** `:yang.python/antlr` and `:yang.python/legacy` are distinct ids. On Node and Dart, where no ANTLR artifacts exist, selecting the ANTLR frontend answers a qualified unavailable-parser outcome.\n   132\t\n   133\t**What Python's profile pins.**\n   134\t\n   135\t| Part | Pin |\n   136\t|---|---|\n   137\t| Grammar | `grammar-id` (`parser.clj:16-19`), entry rules, export profile `:yang.cst/v1` |\n   138\t| Lowering | declared revision plus golden-corpus digest (host code has no content address) |\n   139\t| Runtime | `py` and `pysp` manifest addresses, safepoint profile map, host-module profile addresses (cell, data, integer with limits, stream poll), permitted effects |\n   140\t| Support | derived from `unsupported-rules` (`lower.cljc:78`); reference runtime CPython 3.9.6 |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc | sed -n '250,310p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc | sed -n '25,82p;510,554p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1410,1455p'; rg -n 'site|manifest|primitive|dependency-binding' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | tail -25\"","aggregated_output":"69e58662407223700240cb06877b21c3a6f59db3\n   250\t    (scan ast)))\n   251\t\n   252\t\n   253\t(defn- free-of\n   254\t  \"The free names of the map AST `ast` (`yin.vm/free-names`).\"\n   255\t  [ast]\n   256\t  (let [tree (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast)]\n   257\t    (vm/free-names (query/relation (vals (:rows tree)))\n   258\t                   (query/relation (vm/occurrences tree))\n   259\t                   (:root tree))))\n   260\t\n   261\t\n   262\t(def no-op-tree\n   263\t  \"The tree of a module that collects no program, which is the module of\n   264\t   an empty export list: one `nil` literal, defining nothing.\"\n   265\t  {:type :literal :value nil})\n   266\t\n   267\t\n   268\t(defn- sequenced\n   269\t  \"One tree running `asts` in order: `((fn [_] second) first)`, nested.\n   270\t   A program root in operand position is not a tail call.  No programs\n   271\t   is `no-op-tree`.\"\n   272\t  [asts]\n   273\t  (if (empty? asts)\n   274\t    no-op-tree\n   275\t    (reduce (fn [body ast]\n   276\t              {:type :application\n   277\t               :tail? true\n   278\t               :operator {:type :lambda :params '[_] :body body}\n   279\t               :operands [(cond-> ast\n   280\t                            (= :application (:type ast)) (assoc :tail? false))]})\n   281\t            (peek asts)\n   282\t            (rseq (pop asts)))))\n   283\t\n   284\t\n   285\t(defn- free-declaration\n   286\t  \"How the module declares the free name `n`: `[:requires module\n   287\t   address]`, `[:primitives n profile]`,