{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | sed -n '20,40p'; nl -ba docs/design/yang.antlr.md | sed -n '455,485p'; rg -n 'manifest-address|:yin.module/requires|primitives|profile' src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc | head -35; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc | sed -n '30,80p'\"","aggregated_output":"    20\t   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n    21\t3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n    22\t4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n    23\t\n    24\t## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n    25\t\n    26\t- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n    27\t- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n    28\t- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n    29\t- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n    30\t- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n    31\t- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n    32\t\n    33\t## Tuples and Datoms\n    34\t\n    35\tTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\n    36\tA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\n    37\tSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n    38\t\n    39\tSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n    40\t\n   455\ta suitable runtime is available and through an explicitly configured\n   456\tservice otherwise.\n   457\t\n   458\tANTLR has a Java-based generation tool and target runtimes including\n   459\tJava, JavaScript, TypeScript, and Dart. Target feature parity is not\n   460\tautomatic. Generation and runtime execution are separate concerns\n   461\t(ANTLR target documentation,\n   462\thttps://github.com/antlr/antlr4/blob/dev/doc/targets.md).\n   463\t\n   464\tThe proposed deployment policy is:\n   465\t\n   466\t```text\n   467\t+---------------+-------------------------------+--------------------------+\n   468\t| Consumer host | Local parser implementation   | Alternative              |\n   469\t+===============+===============================+==========================+\n   470\t| JVM / CLJ     | Generated Java parser in an   | Configured parsing       |\n   471\t|               | isolated worker or worker     | service                  |\n   472\t|               | process                       |                          |\n   473\t+---------------+-------------------------------+--------------------------+\n   474\t| Node / CLJS   | Generated JavaScript parser   | Local sidecar or remote  |\n   475\t|               | in a worker; TypeScript       | service                  |\n   476\t|               | compiled at build             |                          |\n   477\t+---------------+-------------------------------+--------------------------+\n   478\t| Dart / CLJD   | Optional generated Dart       | Sidecar where supported, |\n   479\t|               | parser in an isolate          | or a remote service;     |\n   480\t|               |                               | otherwise unsupported    |\n   481\t|               |                               | outcome                  |\n   482\t+---------------+-------------------------------+--------------------------+\n   483\t```\n   484\t\n   485\tA CLJD application can compile through a service without bundling ANTLR.\n48:  \"Derive the manifest footprint from tree rows, declared profiles and dependencies.\"\n49:  [handle {:keys [ast tree primitives requires]}]\