ions; they do not mutate previous source.\n703:Recovery replays the immutable source snapshot. Because a seal fixes the\n712:- Request, source revision, and grammar profile identity.\n773:portable code: it names the source unit revision, the frontend revision\n985:### 7.4 Provenance and fingerprinting\n995:This is separate from the original source-file revision. Record the link\n   346\t\n   347\tThis is not namespace-load registration or mutation of a global\n   348\tregistry. There is no closed language registry anywhere in Yang core.\n   349\t\n   350\tThe composition supplies the catalog to the compiler coordinator. A\n   351\trequest selects a frontend revision and dialect explicitly. Filename\n   352\textensions and content detection can propose a frontend, but ambiguous\n   353\tdetection produces a diagnostic or a choice record rather than silently\n   354\tchoosing.\n   355\t\n   356\tIn-flight requests pin a catalog snapshot. Installing a newer frontend\n   357\tdoes not change their behavior.\n   358\t\n   359\tA missing frontend, missing entry rule, incompatible SPI, unknown required\n   360\toption, or unavailable parser implementation produces a qualified\n   361\tcompiler outcome. None causes automatic dependency downloading or\n   362\texecutable plugin loading.\n   363\t\n   364\t### 3.4 Support profiles\n   433\t\n   434\t### 3.7 Versioning\n   435\t\n   436\tFour things are versioned independently and pinned together per request:\n   437\t\n   438\t- The SPI revision (`:yang.frontend/spi`).\n   439\t- The frontend package revision (`:yang.frontend/revision`), which is\n   440\t  immutable once published.\n   441\t- The grammar package content address and its export profile.\n   442\t- The lowering, runtime, support, and options-schema profiles, each by\n   443\t  content address.\n   444\t\n   445\tTwo dialect packages, or two revisions of one frontend, coexist in one\n   446\tcomposition without registration collisions (Phase 4 exit criterion).\n