{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-02 20:55:16 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-03 03:55:16 +0700\n\nRead the complete C4 design and float64-carrier ruling; checked the relevant source against master `69e58662`. Read-only; no files changed or tests run.\n\n1. **CONCUR — Pure install, instantiate in the importing task.** Export code and immutable specification data; allocate Python namespaces, classes, hooks, and runtime cells only during explicit task-local initialization. Require `py/init!` to initialize the prelude’s own active module store once, with defined failure behavior. Imported bodies execute under the importing task’s effects and may themselves suspend; “no code-delivery wait” must not become a promise that body execution cannot park.\n\n2. **CONCUR — Builtins as a namespace dict.** Maintain one initialized builtin namespace per Python task/runtime instance, shared across its modules. Global lookup checks the module namespace before its builtin fallback, while interpreter-generated exceptions retain canonical builtin class references despite guest rebinding. Test initialization twice, builtin mutation, global shadow/delete, and cross-module exception identity.\n\n3. **CONCUR — Mangle names now.** Reserve `py`, `pysp`, and `pym`; map validated dotted Python names injectively to `pym.a$b$c`, preserving the original Python name in module metadata. Never install a module at the `pym` namespace root. File the registry-prefix issue with tests for both installation orders: [module.cljc:51](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:51) treats encountering a module entry as export traversal, while [module.cljc:69](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:69) installs through the same nested map.\n\n4. **DECLINE — Unqualified hoisting is not semantically invisible.** Accept eager, pinned delivery only as an explicit initial support restriction. `if False: import missing`, catchable failed imports, and a pre-populated `sys.modules` distinguish it from Python statement-time import. The claim at [C4 findings:33](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790974400000-architect-python-c4-design.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:33) therefore needs correction. Required module names are derivable from rows; their manifest addresses additionally require the pinned resolution snapshot. Preserve lazy body execution, but do not claim general Python import semantics until decision 8’s dependency treatment is implemented.\n\n5. **CONCUR — Keep bundled mode through the migration gate, then remove it.** Require four evaluators on three hosts, including publication, parserless consumption, runtime initialization, and exception parity—not merely local evaluation. **Float64 interaction:** first complete carrier preservation and the execution bridge, then rebuild affected rows, derived images, manifests, and references. Linked-prelude migration changes bundled roots separately; neither transition permits aliases from old integer-misclassified content to corrected float content.\n\n6. **CONCUR — AST walker support is mandatory at the migration gate.** L-b must admit the linked prelude’s declared sibling reads through the same verification and store-isolation contract as the other evaluators. Three-VM intermediate development is acceptable; three-VM completion is not. Include cross-module continuation-based exception handling, and make D7 slice C a prerequisite wherever that test exposes incorrect store-context restoration.\n\n7. **CONCUR — Profile-addressed host-module requirements.** L-a must cover publication, requirement discovery, installation matching, effect declarations, and qualified host-export resolution—not merely populate a manifest field. Integer limits and semantic versions must contribute to the matched profile identity; a descriptive `module-version` is insufficient. **Float64 interaction:** capability to execute and re-encode float64 carriers must be checked without changing Jing’s existing numeric bytes or introducing Python-aware codec logic.\n\n8. **DECLINE — `try-require` alone does not close I4.** Adopt the refusal-as-data operation, but add an explicit deferred-dependency contract: check `sys.modules` first, attempt delivery at the import statement under the pinned resolution environment, and access exports only after success. The eager required dependency at [C4 findings:26](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790974400000-architect-python-c4-design.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:26) can fail before the body reaches `try`; changing refusal delivery alone cannot repair that. I4 remains gated until publication/free-name validation can represent these deferred dependencies, including absent names, without weakening address verification or admission.\n\n9. **CONCUR — Refuse cycles initially; use SCC delivery units later.** The I5 publisher must eliminate intra-SCC `require` edges and direct those references to bodies inside the common unit; thin aliases depend on the SCC, never conversely. Specify deterministic member ordering, collision-free export names, and outgoing pins. Preserve separate Python module objects and lazy initialization within the SCC. No new SCC identity primitive is necessary, but its canonical construction still needs a published contract and byte/address fixtures.\n\n10. **CONCUR — Restrict C4 dynamic import names.** Accept only statically recognized calls with literal names under the declared import profile; reject unsupported dynamic use explicitly. This is a scope restriction, not proof that a computed name necessarily implies an unpinned dependency: a future resolver can interpret computed names against an immutable catalog. Rebinding or aliasing `import_module` must not accidentally trigger compiler recognition of the wrong callable.\n\n11. **CONCUR — Limited `importlib`, with precise reload semantics.** Support literal `import_module` and same-image reload; retain the module object and dictionary, preserve names not overwritten, and propagate execution failures without treating reload as a failed first import. Existing external bindings remain unchanged. [Python reload contract](https://docs.python.org/3.9/library/importlib.html#importlib.reload). Switching a name snapshot is insufficient to replace a live task’s cached image automatically; code upgrades need a fresh process or a separately designed replacement protocol. Hooks and path-based discovery remain explicitly unsupported.\n\n12. **CONCUR — Static relative resolution under a declared restriction.** Resolve using both the declared module name and package status: package `a.b` and ordinary module `a.b` have different relative bases. Packageless and beyond-top-level cases raise at the executed statement. Document that modifying `__package__` or supplying a different runtime execution package does not retarget these compiled imports; otherwise static resolution would silently disagree with Python’s runtime package context.\n\n13. **CONCUR — Defer automatic submodule import.** `from pkg import name` must first succeed when the package already exposes that attribute; raise only when attribute lookup fails and the unsupported auto-import fallback would be needed. Preserve parent-child attribute installation for explicitly imported submodules. Also correct the simplified star-import description at [C4 findings:58](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790974400000-architect-python-c4-design.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:58): respect `__all__`, otherwise exclude underscore-prefixed names; copying every ordered key is insufficient.\n\n14. **DECLINE — Publisher-index datoms alone are not an adequate safepoint contract.** Keep sites outside canonical source rows, but publish a content-addressed site set and a pinned association to the exact source root, frontend revision, and transformation profile, with retrieval and verification obligations. The existing transformer already pins the sorted site-set address ([safepoint.cljc:22](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/safepoint.cljc:22)). A separate publication record may avoid changing the module manifest schema, but that is conditional on complete discovery—not an advance prohibition on schema changes. Permit uninstrumented imports only in an explicitly noninterruptible profile; a profile requiring hooks must refuse missing sites.\n\n15. **CONCUR — Keep frontend marks.** Preserve occurrence-scoped marks through publication and transformation; do not infer loop/function meaning from generic lambda structure. Mark imported Python bodies while excluding runtime internals under the selected policy. Correct the atomicity inference at [C4 findings:110](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790974400000-architect-python-c4-design.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:110): an import invokes guest code, which can contain effects and safepoints. Omitting marks from the prelude does not make the complete import operation atomic.\n\n16. **CONCUR — Immutable session catalog, distinct frontend IDs, no fallback.** Pin the selected revision and installed binding for each request; keep language-specific parsing and completeness logic out of REPL core. Return unavailable-parser only when neither a local parser nor an explicitly configured service is available—Node/Dart service compilation is already allowed by [yang.antlr.md:485](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:485). Derive support claims from explicit semantic/runtime declarations and tests, not solely the syntactic `unsupported-rules` list.\n\n17. **DECLINE — A golden-corpus digest does not identify the lowering implementation.** Require an immutable implementation/package revision tied to source or build-artifact digests, its dependencies/options, and a content-addressed lowering-profile descriptor. Keep the golden digest as additional verification evidence. The weaker proposal at [C4 findings:138](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790974400000-architect-python-c4-design.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:138) does not satisfy the independent profile pinning in [yang.antlr.md:436](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:436). Host code need not be executable UAST to have immutable artifact identity.\n\n18. **CONCUR — Persistent `__main__`; reset creates a new Python process.** Keep its module object, globals dictionary, builtin namespace, and import cache across submissions. Reset must create fresh task-owned runtime state, including handler stacks and hook attachments, while immutable verified code may be reused. Do not merge runtime state across frontend revisions or let late results from the old process mutate the new one.\n\n19. **CONCUR — Gate foreign-principal imports on D7 slice B and its tests.** Verify closure origin/store ownership recursively through exports, re-exports, and transitive dependency slices before exposing executable values. B is necessary, not sufficient: the claim also requires authenticated publication, dependency-pin checks, primitive/effect admission, and working store-context transitions on all four VMs. **Float64 interaction:** corrected publication must rebuild affected derivations, manifests, and dependency pins before cross-principal address acceptance.\n\n**Address clarification applying throughout:** the linked user-code root can remain stable across prelude revisions only when its emitted rows and ABI references remain unchanged. The published **module manifest does not** remain stable when its prelude dependency pin changes: `:yin.module/requires` is part of the addressed manifest ([publish.cljc:184](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:184)). Thus [C4 findings:115](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790974400000-architect-python-c4-design.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:115) must distinguish code-root stability from executable package identity. Float-carrier correction and linked-prelude migration must each preserve this distinction.\n\n### Converged rulings\n\n1. Install only code and immutable specifications; instantiate Python and hook state explicitly in the consuming task.\n2. Use one task-local builtin namespace with global fallback and canonical references for interpreter-generated exceptions.\n3. Reserve `py`, `pysp`, and `pym`; use injective Python-name mangling pending the registry-prefix fix.\n4. Permit hoisted pinned delivery only under an explicit eager-dependency restriction; keep module-body execution lazy.\n5. Retain bundled mode until twelve-lane migration and float64 address gates pass, then remove it and rebuild affected artifacts.\n6. Require AST-walker linked-prelude support and correct cross-module store-context restoration before migration completion.\n7. Declare and enforce host-export semantic profiles, including integer limits, throughout publication and linking.\n8. Add refusal-as-data linking together with deferred dependency admission and statement-time import resolution before claiming catchable missing imports.\n9. Refuse delivery cycles initially; later publish deterministic SCC units with internal body references and outward-only aliases.\n10. Restrict C4 import names to supported static literal forms; diagnose dynamic forms without misrecognizing rebound callables.\n11. Support literal `import_module` and same-image reload with retained namespace semantics; defer hooks, path discovery, and live code replacement.\n12. Resolve relative imports from declared module/package context and disclose the restriction on runtime package retargeting.\n13. Defer missing-attribute submodule auto-import while preserving existing attributes, parent bindings, and correct star-import filtering.\n14. Publish pinned, retrievable, verifiable safepoint site artifacts; refuse missing sites when instrumentation is required.\n15. Retain occurrence-scoped frontend marks and distinguish runtime-internal atomicity from imported guest-code execution.\n16. Pin immutable frontend catalogs and revisions, prohibit fallback, and allow explicitly configured parser services on every host.\n17. Pin lowering implementation artifacts and dependencies through a content-addressed profile; use corpus digests as supplementary evidence.\n18. Preserve `__main__` across submissions; reset creates fresh Python task state and isolates outstanding old-process work.\n19. Claim foreign-principal imports only after D7 ownership checks and the complete publication, dependency, profile, and execution gates pass."}}
